* START-HISTORY:
* 19 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 346, from Linux ce649c1a: THE PERSON LOADS THEIR OWN (owner, 19 Nov 2026).  With no account named the
*               examples go into the account the session is in, for anybody who has bp.edit (12413 otherwise, before anything is written); naming
*               accounts is the administrator's, and a suspended one named is said and skipped.  No longer run by the installer or by create.account,
*               and no form loads every account.  In every account's VOC.  Run in a person's own account, the compiler's two pointers go into that
*               account's VOC (the session's own) for the length of the compile, as they go into SDSYS's for the administrator's.
* 17 Nov 26 Windows port - Linux 1557e270's reply ("close it in the verb"): when it runs for SDSYS, its examples.bp and examples.bp.out are given bp.out's
*               rights (SYSTEM and Administrators full, sdusers read; secure-sysdirs.ps1's directory grants) through !ps_script (lock.sdsys); they inherited
*               sdsys's sdusers Modify.  A failure is said with 10055.
* 16 Nov 26 Windows port - from Linux 657da3de (S.261) and cc7e41c6: new.  NO LOGTO: each account's examples.bp is reached by the pathname the register holds,
*               as LOAD.HELP reaches the shared account (RELEASE_1.1 187), because on this port LOGTO out of SDSYS gives up the administrator right and an
*               installer's internal session cannot LOGTO back (RELEASE_1.1 310 lets only a desktop SDSYS sign-in do that).  The compile is given two
*               pointers of SDSYS's own VOC to the account's examples.bp and examples.bp.out and removes them again; the account's own VOC gets the two
*               relative pointers a file made in the account would have.  Messages 14121-14126 are 15121-15126 here.
* 17 Nov 26 dm  The verb takes a LIST of accounts (the owner, 17 Nov 2026: "both load.examples and remove.examples should take a list of accounts"); an unknown name is
*               said (14122) and skipped instead of ending the command.  Its companion is REMOVE.EXAMPLES (removeexamples).
* 17 Nov 26 dm  New (the owner, 17 Nov 2026: "there should be a examples.bp file available to all users that contains those programs and a set of
*               training programs typical for a Computer Science 101 course", then "all four versions should deliver with examples.bp").  Asked how
*               every account reaches it - the shared account's lookup serves no program file (S.66), and an F-pointer outside an account is refused by
*               the OS gate - he chose "A copy in every account": no gate changes.  Made from loadhelp, which fills context.help the same way.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   LOAD.EXAMPLES                       the account this session is in - anybody whose account has bp.edit (the administrator always may)
*   LOAD.EXAMPLES account ...           the administrator's: the accounts named, a list; a name that is not registered is said and skipped,
*                                       and the others go on
*
*   Run by a person for their own account (the command, or the home screen's Example programs tile), or by the administrator for accounts
*   named, and again after the programs under examples change.  Nothing loads them into an account by itself.  Every file of the
*   examples directory of the SD system directory (C:\ProgramData\SD\sdsys\examples) is one program, the FILE NAME its record id (a lower case
*   letter first, then a-z 0-9 dot dash underscore, up to 80 characters): the five programs of the SD Text User Interface - Programmer's Guide
*   (hello, menus, notes, browse, stock) and the training programs cs01 to cs23.  In each account they go into examples.bp, a directory file of the
*   account, made when it is not there; a record that is already the same is not written; a program under an id that is not shipped is kept; a
*   shipped one is replaced, so a change to a shipped program is made under examples, not in the file.  Then each program that is new, changed, or
*   has no object yet is compiled there, so the account's person can run it where it is (RUN examples.bp name) or copy it to bp to change it.
*   "Compiled N of M" counts those M.
*
*   NO GATE IS TOUCHED: examples.bp is an ordinary program file of the account (a name ending .bp), so the edit gate bp.edit decides whether its
*   person may load or change it - off as delivered.  It does not LOGTO (on Windows a LOGTO out of SDSYS ends the administrator
*   right): it reaches each account's directory by the pathname in the register and never leaves the account it runs in.  An account it cannot reach or make the file
*   in is named and skipped; a program that does not compile is named.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
program load.examples
$catalog $loadexamples

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h
$include err.h

deffun ps_script(script) calling '!ps_script'

   @system.return.code = -ER$ARGS
*  19 Nov 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 346, from Linux ce649c1a (S.269 follow-up).  THE PERSON LOADS THEIR OWN (owner, 19 Nov 2026: "wouldnt
*  it be easier to just have the user load the examples rather than do it for every account.  most accounts will not need the examples", then
*  of the edit gate, "respect the gate").  With no account named the examples go into the account this session is in, for anybody; naming
*  accounts is the administrator's.  THE EDIT GATE IS ASKED HERE, BEFORE ANYTHING IS WRITTEN: this program is $internal, so the kernel would
*  let its writes into examples.bp through, and the compile would then be refused by BASIC half way; so an account without bp.edit is refused
*  at once in BASIC's own words (12413, Linux's 11408) and nothing changes.
   is.admin = kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1)
   if dcount(trim(@sentence), ' ') > 1 and not(is.admin) then
      stop sysmsg(2001) ;* Command requires administrator privileges
   end
   if not(is.admin) and not(kernel(K$BP.EDIT, -1)) then
      @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
      stop sysmsg(12413) ;* Programs can only be compiled or cataloged by an account that has bp.edit (SDSYS can give it: modify.account <account> bp-on)
   end

   keychars = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789._-'
   bs = char(92)
   src = @sdsys : @ds : 'examples'
   entries = dir(src)
   if entries = '' then stop sysmsg(15121, src)                   ;* The example programs directory is missing or empty: %1

*  The programs, read once: names and texts
   names = '' ; texts = ''
   n.bad = 0
   for i = 1 to dcount(entries, @fm)
      name = entries<i, 1>
      if entries<i, 2> # 'F' then continue
      gosub read.one
   next i
   if names = '' then stop sysmsg(15121, src)

*  The accounts: those named (a list, each once; the administrator's), or the account this session is in.  A name that is not registered is said
*  and skipped.  19 Nov 26: with no name it is no longer every account in the register (the owner: "most accounts will not need the examples").
   want = downcase(trim(@sentence))                              ;* the verb, then the account names
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'accounts' to acc.f else stop sysmsg(6003)
   accts = ''
   if dcount(want, ' ') = 1 then
      accts = downcase(@who)
   end else
      for w = 2 to dcount(want, ' ')
         aid = field(want, ' ', w)
         read arec from acc.f, aid else
            display sysmsg(15122, aid)                           ;* No account is registered as %1 ...
            n.bad += 1
            continue
         end
*        19 Nov 26 (Linux ce649c1a): a SUSPENDED account named is said and skipped, whatever the session is
         if upcase(trim(arec<ACC$SUSPENDED>)) = 'SUSPENDED' then
            display sysmsg(15123, aid)                           ;* Could not enter the account %1, or make the file examples.bp there ...
            n.bad += 1
            continue
         end
         locate aid in accts<1> by 'AL' setting ap else ins aid before accts<ap>
      next w
   end

*  The session's own VOC (SDSYS's for the administrator, the account's own for a person) holds, for the length of each account's compile, two pointers to that account's examples.bp and examples.bp.out: the compiler opens
*  its source and object files by VOC name, and this session never leaves SDSYS.
   open 'VOC' to sv else stop sysmsg(2026)                       ;* Cannot open VOC
   src.voc = 'load.examples.src'
   gosub drop.pointers

   n.acc = 0 ; n.new = 0 ; n.changed = 0 ; n.same = 0 ; n.kept = 0 ; n.compiled = 0 ; n.tried = 0
   for a = 1 to dcount(accts, @fm)
      acct = accts<a>
      gosub do.account
   next a
   gosub drop.pointers
   close sv
   close acc.f

   void kernel(K$AUDIT, 'load.examples accounts=' : n.acc : ' new=' : n.new : ' changed=' : n.changed : ' same=' : n.same : ' kept=' : n.kept : ' compiled=' : n.compiled : ' of=' : n.tried : ' skipped=' : n.bad)
   display sysmsg(15124, n.acc, n.new, n.changed, n.same)           ;* SYSMSG takes four places
   display sysmsg(15125, n.compiled, n.tried, n.kept)
   @system.return.code = (if n.bad then -ER$FAILED else 0)
   stop

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- one account

do.account:
   acct.dir = ''
   read arec from acc.f, acct then gosub account.dir
   if acct.dir = '' then
      display sysmsg(15123, acct)                                ;* Could not enter the account %1 ...
      n.bad += 1
      return
   end
   x.path = acct.dir : '/examples.bp'
   o.path = x.path : '.out'
   openpath x.path to xf else
      made = @true
      create.file x.path directory on error
         made = @false
      end
      if made then
         openpath x.path to xf else made = @false
      end
      if not(made) then
         display sysmsg(15123, acct)
         n.bad += 1
         return
      end
   end
   have.obj = @false
   openpath o.path to of then
      have.obj = @true
   end else
      made = @true
      create.file o.path directory on error
         made = @false
      end
      if made then
         openpath o.path to of else made = @false
      end
      if not(made) then
         close xf
         display sysmsg(15123, acct)
         n.bad += 1
         return
      end
   end
*  The account's own VOC names both files, relative, as a file made in the account names them (a pointer already there is left as it is)
   openpath convert('/', bs, acct.dir) : bs : 'voc' to avf else
      close xf ; close of
      display sysmsg(15123, acct)
      n.bad += 1
      return
   end
   for vi = 1 to 2
      vid = (if vi = 1 then 'examples.bp' else 'examples.bp.out')
      read vrec from avf, vid else
         vrec = (if vi = 1 then 'F File of example programs' else 'F')
         vrec<2> = vid
         write vrec to avf, vid
      end
   next vi
   close avf
   n.acc += 1
   todo = ''                                                     ;* the programs to compile here: new, changed, or with no object yet
   for p = 1 to dcount(names, @fm)
      rec = raise(texts<p>)
      old = ''
      read old from xf, names<p> then
         if old = rec then
            n.same += 1
            obj = ''
            if have.obj then read obj from of, names<p> else obj = ''
            if obj = '' then todo<-1> = names<p>
            continue
         end
         n.changed += 1
      end else
         n.new += 1
      end
      todo<-1> = names<p>
      wok = @true
      write rec to xf, names<p> on error wok = @false
      if not(wok) then
         display sysmsg(15126, acct : ' ' : names<p>, 'it could not be written')
         n.bad += 1
      end
   next p
   select xf to 12
   loop
      readnext rid from 12 else exit
      locate rid in names<1> setting kp else n.kept += 1
   repeat
   close xf
   close of
*  Each program to compile, one at a time, judged on the compiler's own success line, through SDSYS's two pointers to this account's files
   pv = 'F' : @fm : convert('/', bs, x.path)
   write pv to sv, src.voc
   pv<2> = convert('/', bs, o.path)
   write pv to sv, src.voc : '.out'
   for p = 1 to dcount(todo, @fm)
      n.tried += 1
      execute 'basic ' : src.voc : ' ' : todo<p> capturing out
      if index(out, 'Compiled 1 program(s) with no errors', 1) then
         n.compiled += 1
      end else
         display sysmsg(15126, acct : ' ' : todo<p>, 'it did not compile')
         n.bad += 1
      end
   next p
   gosub drop.pointers
   if acct = 'sdsys' then gosub lock.sdsys
   return

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- SDSYS's own two files, closed in the verb

* 17 Nov 26 Windows port - Linux 1557e270 asked it closed in the verb ("close it in the verb").  SDSYS's examples.bp and examples.bp.out are in sdsys, SDSYS's
*  account directory, so a file this verb makes there inherits the folder's inherit-only sdusers Modify (found on the fresh install of 17 Nov, icacls), where
*  bp.out beside them reads sdusers (RX).  Each time it runs for SDSYS both are given bp.out's rights - SYSTEM and Administrators full, sdusers read - with the
*  grants secure-sysdirs.ps1 gives a directory (sd.iss LockSdsysExamples, after the installer's LOAD.EXAMPLES, does the same through that script), through
*  !ps_script like create_account's secure.account.dir, so an unelevated SDSYS session does it through its elevated helper.  SDSYS's own later runs keep
*  their write through Administrators.  A failure is named (10055) and counted; the programs stay loaded.  Single-quoted paths: PowerShell does not expand them.
lock.sdsys:
   q = char(39)
   for li = 1 to 2
      lp = convert('/', bs, (if li = 1 then x.path else o.path))
      ls.ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
      ls.ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
      ls.ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '
      ls.ps := '& icacls.exe ' : q : change(lp, q, q:q) : q : ' /inheritance:r'
      ls.ps := ' /grant "*S-1-5-18:(OI)(CI)(F)" /grant "*S-1-5-32-544:(OI)(CI)(F)" /grant "sdusers:(OI)(CI)(RX)" | Out-Null; '
      ls.ps := 'exit $LASTEXITCODE'
      ls.rc = ps_script(ls.ps)
      if ls.rc # 0 then
         display sysmsg(10055, lp, ls.rc)                        ;* Could not secure account directory %1 (%2)
         n.bad += 1
      end
   next li
   return

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- the account's directory, from its register record

*  The register may hold an @SDSYS token, a drive letter path with forward slashes (C:/ProgramData/SD/user_accounts/fred) or backslashes; the answer
*  is in forward slashes, without a trailing one, or a null string when it is not an absolute path.
account.dir:
   acct.dir = trim(arec<ACC$PATH>)
   if acct.dir[1, 1] = '@' then acct.dir = parse.pathname.tokens(acct.dir)
   acct.dir = convert(bs, '/', acct.dir)
   loop
   while len(acct.dir) > 3 and acct.dir[1] = '/' do
      acct.dir = acct.dir[1, len(acct.dir) - 1]
   repeat
   if len(acct.dir) < 2 or not(acct.dir[2, 1] = ':' or acct.dir[1, 1] = '/') then acct.dir = ''
   return

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- SDSYS's two pointers for the compiler

drop.pointers:
   for di = 1 to 2
      did = (if di = 1 then src.voc else src.voc : '.out')
      read drec from sv, did then delete sv, did
   next di
   return

* ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- one shipped file

read.one:
   ok = (len(name) >= 1 and len(name) <= 80 and index('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz', name[1, 1], 1) > 0)
   if ok then
      for ci = 2 to len(name)
         if index(keychars, name[ci, 1], 1) = 0 then ok = 0
      next ci
   end
   if not(ok) then
      display sysmsg(15126, name, 'not a key')
      n.bad += 1
      return
   end
   rec = ''
   nf = 0
   got = 0
   openseq src : @ds : name readonly to sf then
      got = 1
      loop
         readseq ln from sf else exit
         nf += 1
         rec<nf> = trimb(ln)
      repeat
      close sf
   end
   if not(got) or nf = 0 then
      display sysmsg(15126, name, 'cannot be read')
      n.bad += 1
      return
   end
   locate name in names<1> by 'AL' setting np else
      ins name before names<np>
      ins lower(rec) before texts<np>
   end
   return

end

* END-CODE
